; minimal
false ; never load this theory, this file is just for reference
      ; it is already hard-coded in `kurt.py`, look for `initial_kb`

;; syntax
brackets ( )                                 ; for grouping during parsing
infix    ,    5  4                           ; for lists and tuples, right associative: `(a, b, c)` is `(a, (b, c))`
infix    " " 90 90                           ; space for function applications, binds most tightly

const true                                   ; declare constant symbol
bool  true                                   ; true has type bool
infix implies 13 12                          ; logical implies, right associative (since 13>12)
infix and     16 16                          ; and operator
bool  implies 0 1 2, and 0 1 2               ; both take and give booleans
flat  and                                    ; conjunctions need no nesting
sym   and                                    ; `A and B` is the same as `B and A`
alias ⊤ true
alias ⇒ implies
alias ∧ and
; `EXPR local "label"` marks a label as not exported by `load` -- a special parse rule, no
; declaration (see `local_led` in `kurt.py`)

;; inference rules genuinely hard-coded in `kurt.py` (not `use` axioms you could remove)
;
; - "top-intro": `true` is always derivable -- see `derive_expr`
; - "impl-elim" (modus ponens): given `$A` and `$A implies $B` in the theory, `$B` is
;   derivable -- see `impl_elim`; this also covers plain "restating" an already-proven
;   fact, via `impl_elim`'s premise-less-implication case (case 2 there)
; - "and-intro": given `$A` and `$B` separately provable, `$A and $B` is derivable, and
;   a claimed conjunction is split into its conjuncts, each derived separately -- see
;   `eval_expression` and the conjunction-splitting case in `derive_expr`
; - "impl-intro" / "not-intro": *not* standalone axioms here -- they are the effect of
;   closing an `assume` block (see `eval_done`'s 'assume' case): assuming `$A` and
;   deriving `$B` gives `$A implies $B`; additionally deriving `false` gives `not $A`
;
; NOT hard-coded, despite once being drafted here: a general "restatement" schema
; (`$A implies $A`) and a general "impl-intro" schema
; (`($A implies $B) implies ($A implies $B)`) as bare axioms -- neither is reachable as
; a standalone fact (e.g. a bare `A implies A` does not derive from nothing). Prove a
; specific instance instead, with `show`/`proof`/`assume`/`qed` (see `tutorial/`,
; lessons 08-proof and 18-assume). `not`/`false` themselves also aren't declared here at
; all -- see `prop.kurt` for those, and for a general, always-available "not-intro".

;; names the engine itself knows, although they are declared in theories
;
; the engine treats these symbols specially *by name*, once some theory declares them:
; - `iff`: every fact `A iff B` is also used as `A implies B` and `B implies A` (`impl_elim`)
; - `not`, `false`: closing an `assume` block whose last line is `false` also gives `not A`
; - `=`, `iff`: the only relations `def` accepts (and only from the packaged `equality.kurt` /
;   `prop.kurt`); their two sides are never reordered, although both are `sym`
; - `+ - * / ^` and `= ≠ < <= > >=` on numbers: computed with `calc on`
; - `sub`, `forall`, `exists`: see below, and the rules behind `let` ("forall-intro") and
;   `pick` ("exists-elim")

;; substitution operator
arity  sub 3                                 ; operator for substitutions
bindop sub                                   ; first arg must be a variable that is bound

;; forall/exists syntax (their axioms -- forall-elim, exists-intro -- still live in
;; logic.kurt, and still need `load logic`; only the *syntax* is hard-coded, so that the
;; two lines below aren't the only thing standing between "forall" meaning something and
;; it crashing the theory-storage machinery the moment anyone uses the word -- see
;; `theory_append`/`remove_outer_forall_quantifiers` in `kurt.py`)
arity  forall 2, exists 2
bindop forall, exists
bool   forall 0 2, exists 0 2                ; position 2 (the body) must be boolean
const  forall, exists
alias  ∀ forall
alias  ∃ exists

; for automatic testing we add the following line (in this case we should fail, because of `false`):
;;; ProofError: can not derive `false`
